#### Problem 2: Complex Client-Side Orchestration Logic

**Scenario**: Provider portal displays claim detail page requiring data from Claims Service (claim data), Provider Service (rendering provider), Member Service (member demographics), Procedure Service (procedure codes), and Diagnosis Service (diagnosis codes).

**Client Code Complexity**:
```javascript
// Complex orchestration with error handling
async function loadClaimDetail(claimId) {
  try {
    // Step 1: Get claim data
    const claim = await fetchWithRetry('/claims/' + claimId);

    // Step 2: Get related entities in parallel (depends on claim data)
    const [provider, member, procedures, diagnoses] = await Promise.all([
      fetchWithRetry('/providers/' + claim.providerId),
      fetchWithRetry('/members/' + claim.memberId),
      fetchWithRetry('/procedures?codes=' + claim.procedureCodes.join(',')),
      fetchWithRetry('/diagnoses?codes=' + claim.diagnosisCodes.join(','))
    ]);

    // Step 3: Merge data and handle missing pieces
    return {
      ...claim,
      provider: provider || { name: 'Unknown' },
      member: member || { name: 'Unknown' },
      procedureDetails: procedures || [],
      diagnosisDetails: diagnoses || []
    };
  } catch (error) {
    // Complex error handling for partial failures
    if (error.service === 'provider' && claim) {
      // Degrade gracefully - show claim without provider details
      return { ...claim, provider: null };
    }
    throw error;
  }
}

function fetchWithRetry(url, retries = 3) {
  // Retry logic repeated in every client
  // Circuit breaker logic repeated in every client
  // Timeout handling repeated in every client
}
```

**Impact**:
- 200-300 lines of orchestration code per complex view
- Duplicated logic across web, mobile, and partner client codebases
- Inconsistent error handling and retry strategies
- Difficult to maintain when services change
- Higher client development costs and longer time-to-market

